<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="dec-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="dec-title">Decisions that catch consultants out</h2>
    </header>
    <dl class="fit-list fit-list--wide">
      <div><dt>When do I need GST?</dt><dd>Section 22 of the CGST Act sets ₹20 lakh outside special category States, but some situations need registration regardless. Keep watch on your annual total, and ask before you cross it. See {% routeLink "/services/gst-registration/", "GST registration" %}.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Foreign clients?</dt><dd>Services to clients abroad may be treated as exports for GST if conditions are met, which can involve specific paperwork and how you receive foreign currency. Ask before your first invoice, not after.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Is the presumptive scheme right for me?</dt><dd>Section 58 of the Income-tax Act, 2025 offers 50% of receipts up to ₹50 lakh, or ₹75 lakh if cash receipts are within 5%, for specified professions. Section 62(4) names technical consultancy and information technology, among others, but not consulting in general, so ask whether yours qualifies. Consider your real expenses too.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Advance tax</dt><dd>Clients' TDS may not cover your full tax. Tax may be due in instalments during the year. Set aside a share of each receipt.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Is a company or LLP sensible?</dt><dd>It is often unnecessary at the beginning, and it adds yearly compliance. See {% routeLink "/services/company-llp-registration/", "choosing an entity" %}.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="exp-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="exp-title" class="first-h2">Expenses you should keep proof of</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Laptop, software and subscriptions, with invoices in your name</li>
        <li>Co-working or office rent</li>
        <li>Travel for client work, with a note of the project</li>
        <li>Professional memberships and training</li>
        <li>Internet and phone, with an estimate of work use agreed with your accountant</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Only claim what you can support. If a client reimburses a cost, keep it separate on the invoice and in your records.</p>

      <h2 id="year">A low-effort routine</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>On each invoice:</strong> update the sheet.</li>
        <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> mark what was received and chase overdue invoices.</li>
        <li><strong>Quarterly:</strong> check the portal for TDS credit, and consider advance tax.</li>
        <li><strong>Yearly:</strong> hand over the sheet, statements and bills for your return.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="need">Do you even need an accountant?</h2>
      <p>With one client and one account, perhaps only at return time. Once you have GST, foreign clients or several clients with TDS, regular help usually pays for itself in avoided errors.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Do you work with people in my kind of consulting?</li>
        <li>Will you check TDS credits against invoices?</li>
        <li>Can you handle export invoicing, if I need it?</li>
        <li>Will you remind me about advance tax?</li>
      </ul>

